\end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Motivation}
Endogeneity due to OVB is a pervasive issue.

One approach to address it: instrumental variable regressions

Finding suitable instruments is very hard.

Today we consider an alternative approach to address certain forms of omitted variables: 
\textbf{panel data} allows to control for all \textit{time-invariant} unobservables.

\end{frame}

\section{What is Panel Data?}

\begin{frame}{What is Panel Data?}
Panel data: same cross-sectional unit is observed over multiple time periods 
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{cross-sectional unit} can be a person, company, city, state, etc. ($i = 1,…n$) 
    \item \textit{time period} can be a year, a month, a day, etc. ($t = 1,…,T$)
\end{itemize}

We denote a sample of panel observations as  $\{Y_{it}, \boldsymbol{X}_{it}\}$,  $i = 1, ... , n$, and $t = 1, ..., T$.

\bigskip
\pause
\textbf{Balanced} panel: $\: \: \: \: \{Y_{it}, \boldsymbol{X}_{it}\}$ $i = 1, ..., n; t = 1, . . . , T$  \color{gray}[n x T observations in total] \color{black} \\
\textbf{Unbalanced} panel: $\{Y_{it}, \boldsymbol{X}_{it}\}$, $i = 1, ..., n; t = \underline{t}_i . . . , \overline{t}_i$,
where $\underline{t}_i$ and $\overline{t}_i$ may vary across $i$. $\rightarrow$ we do not observe the same number of periods for each $i$.

Unbalancedness is prevalent in practice, but we will abstract from this for the sake of exposition. 
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{(Unbalanced) Panel Data: Example}
