\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{What is Panel Data?}
Our focus is on large micro-level survey data sets. These are often called “large $n$, small $T$” panels.
\begin{itemize}
    \item many cross-sectional units $n$ (which may be randomly sampled) 
    \item observed for small number of time periods $T$ (which are typically ordered)
\end{itemize} 

\medskip 
We usually assume that the cross-sectional units (e.g households, firms) are randomly sampled from a large population $\rightarrow$ i.i.d. across $i = 1, ... , n$.

At the same time, we will not make the i.i.d assumption within a unit $i$ across time (i.e., for a given unit $i$), as it is typically implausible in most settings.
\vspace{-.3cm}
\small \begin{itemize}
    \item example: income of household $i$ in year $t$ and year $t+1$ 
\end{itemize} 

\end{frame}

\section{Within- and between-Variation}


\begin{frame}{Pooled OLS}
    We can write a linear panel data model  as:
\begin{align*}
   Y_{it} = \boldsymbol{X}_{it} \boldsymbol{\beta} + u_{it} , i = 1, ... , n; t = 1, ... , T. 
\end{align*}

$\boldsymbol{X}_{it}$ includes all regressors. \\
They might vary only across $i$ (e.g. minimum drinking age), only across $t$ (e.g. year dummies) or across both $i$ and $t$ (seat belt usage rate). 


\pause

\color{teal}\textbf{Pooled OLS} \color{black}
= estimate the parameters of the model by OLS

%When computing the OLS estimator, we just sum over both $i$ and $t$. \\
\begin{itemize}

\item  treats the cross-section and time series dimensions of the data in the
same way %, and  $\rightarrow$  

\item exploits $all$ variation in the data, both within and between cross-sectional units.

\item consistent under MLR.1 to MLR.4 \\
    (MLR.2 implies: observations for different $t$ are sampled independently).


\end{itemize} 
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Example: Pooled OLS}
    \includegraphics[width = .8\linewidth]{plot1.png} 
\end{frame}

%\section{Within- vs. between-variation}

\begin{frame}{Within- vs. between-variation}

Pooled OLS treats the whole data as one large (pooled) i.i.d. sample.
\vspace{-0.23cm}
\begin{itemize}
    \item often not justified as observations are not independent within units $i$ across $t$.
    \item moreover, it does not exploit the specific advantages of panel data!
\end{itemize}
\bigskip
    
In a panel data set, we can decompose the total variation in our variables into

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \alert{within variation:} changes within each cross-sectional group $i$ over time.
     \item \alert{between variation:} changes between cross-sectional groups 
\end{enumerate}

Example: seat belt usage rate varies \textit{between} states, and \textit{within} each state across years. \\
minimum drinking age only varies between states. It is \alert{time-invariant}.
\end{frame}


\begin{frame}{The Panel Model}

In the same way, unobservable determinants of $Y_{it}$ in the error include 

\begin{itemize}
    \item time-invariant components: only differ between units $i$ (e.g. geography, population's attitude towards seat belts)  
    \item and components that vary between units $i$ \textit{and} within unit $i$ over time (e.g. weather conditions)
\end{itemize}


Assuming the error term consists of two components, we can write:
\begin{align*}
       Y_{it} = \boldsymbol{X}_{it} \boldsymbol{\beta} + \underbrace{c_i + \epsilon_{it}}_{u_{it}} , i = 1, ... , n; t = 1, ... , T. 
\end{align*}
\vspace{-0.3cm}
\begin{description}
    \item[$c_i$] unobserved time-invariant characteristics of unit $i$.
    \item[$\epsilon_{it}$] idiosyncratic error term, varies both across $i$ and $t$.
\end{description}
\pause 

with \alert{\textbf{$c_i$ potentially correlated with included regressors:}}  $Cov(\boldsymbol{X}_{it},c_i) \neq 0 \: \Rightarrow E[\epsilon_{it}+c_i|\boldsymbol{X}_{it}] \neq 0 \: \rightarrow$ endogeneity, bias in pooled OLS
\end{frame}



\begin{frame}{Within- and between-Variation}
    \includegraphics[width =.8\linewidth]{plot2.png}
\end{frame}


\begin{frame}{Panel Data and Time-Invariant Unobservables}

$c_i$ (= individual \textbf{fixed effect} or unobserved heterogeneity) is fixed over time.


With panel data, we can use \alert{estimators that exploit only the $within-$variation} in the data, thereby eliminating all time-invariant factors!

This can help us address OVB by \alert{controlling for \textit{all time-invariant} error components $c_i$}.


Let's look at different ways to estimate the fixed effects model.
\end{frame}

\section{Panel Data Estimators}
\begin{frame}{The Dummy Variable Regression}

Option 1) explicitly add $c_i$ to our regression by adding a dummy for each cross-sectional unit $i$. 

Example: Using data at the state$\times$year level, we can “control” for a state-dummy (all time-invariant unobservable characteristics at the state level, e.g. local laws).
\begin{align*}
       Y_{it} = \boldsymbol{X}_{it} \boldsymbol{\beta} + \only<1>{ \alpha_1 \mathbbm{1}\{state = i\} + ... + \alpha_n \mathbbm{1}\{state = n\}} 
       \only<2>{ \color{blue} \underbrace{\alpha_1 \mathbbm{1}\{state = 1\}}_{c_1} + ... + \underbrace{\alpha_n \mathbbm{1}\{state = n\}}_{c_n}}\color{black} 
       + \, \epsilon_{it} , \: \: i = 1, ... , n; t = 1, ... , T. 
\end{align*}
\small Note: we either have to omit one dummy, or the constant.


\normalsize
 \texttt{Keeping controls \alert{and the state fixed}, a change in $X_1$ by 1 unit is associated with...} \\
 $\rightarrow$  coefficients are estimated from variation for one specific $i$ over time only ($within-$variation):
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{The Dummy Variable Estimator}
We can estimate the model with the cross-sectional-unit-dummies by  OLS to obtain $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}$. \\
$\rightarrow$ OLS is unbiased if $E[\epsilon_{it}|\boldsymbol{X_{it}}, c_i] = 0$ (MLR.4 for this model).

Because all time-invariant unobservables are eliminated from $\epsilon_{it}$, this is a much less strong assumption than $E[u_{it}|\boldsymbol{X_{it}}] = 0$.

Example: weather conditions much more likely to satisfy conditional mean independence than population's attitude towards seat belts.

When thinking about omitted variables that might be correlated with included regressors, we can ignore all time-invariant unobservables.  Every conceivable time-invariant variable is accounted for in our analyses!
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]{Pooled OLS vs. Dummy Variable Estimator}
<<include = F>>=
library(sandwich)
library(lmtest)
dat <- read.csv("seatbelts.csv")
head(dat)
@
\footnotesize
<<>>=
pooled <- lm(fatality_rate ~ seatbelt_usage, data = dat) 
coeftest(pooled, vcov. = vcovHC(pooled, "HC1"))

@
\end{frame}


\begin{frame}[fragile]{Pooled OLS vs. Dummy Variable Estimator}
\footnotesize
<<>>=
# include state dummies
dvr <- lm(fatality_rate ~ seatbelt_usage + factor(state), data = dat) 
coeftest(dvr, vcov. = vcovHC(dvr, "HC1"))

@
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Interpretation}
    Pooled OLS: \\ Comparing seat belt usage rate and fatality rates \textit{across states and years}, we find that an increase in the seatbelt usage rate by 0.1 (10 percentage points) is associated with an \textit{increase} in the number of fatalities per million miles driven by .00057.  \color{gray} (=2.9\% of the sample mean).

\bigskip
\color{black}

Dummy variable estimation: \\ Comparing seat belt usage rate and fatality rates \textit{within states over time}, we find that an increase in the seat belt usage rate by .1 is associated with a \textit{decrease} in the number of fatalities per million miles driven by .0017.  \color{gray} (=8.6\% of the sample mean).

    
\end{frame}
\begin{frame}{Illustration: Fixed Effects Estimation}
    \includegraphics[width =.8\linewidth]{plot4.png}
\end{frame}


\begin{frame}{Another Example}
Climate change literature: how do rising temperatures affect human health and productivity?

Say we are interested in the effect of outdoor temperature ($X_{it}$) on exam performance ($Y_{it}$) among students in Italy.

We have a \textbf{panel} of students $i = 1,...,n$ all across the country for multiple exams taken at dates $t = 1,...,T$.

Just regressing $Y_{it}$ on $X_{it}$ (pooled OLS) might yield biased estimates, e.g. if students in the warmer South study harder because of bad economic prospects in the South. 

$\rightarrow$ $X_{it}$ correlated with $u_{it}$ (e.g. effort)
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Another Example}
\begin{align*}
       Y_{it} = \boldsymbol{X}_{it} \boldsymbol{\beta} +  \color{blue} \underbrace{\alpha_1 \mathbbm{1}\{student = 1\}}_{c_1} + ... + \underbrace{\alpha_n \mathbbm{1}\{student = n\}}_{c_n}\color{black}
       + \epsilon_{it} , i = 1, ... , n; t = 1, ... , T. 
\end{align*}

Dummy variable regression allows to control for all time-invariant unobservables $c_i$, e.g. overall student effort and ability!

Effect of temperature will be estimated from variation for one specific student over exam dates (within-−student variation). 

\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{First-Differences Estimator}

Option 2) If $T = 2$, an alternative to the dummy variable approach is first-differencing:

Consider the models for the two time periods:
\begin{align*}
    Y_{i1}& = \boldsymbol{X}'_{i1}\boldsymbol{\beta} + c_i + \epsilon_{i1}  \text{ and  } \\
Y_{i2} &= \boldsymbol{X}'_{i2}\boldsymbol{\beta} + c_i + \epsilon_{i2}   
\end{align*}
Differencing yields: 
\begin{align*}
Y_{i2} – Y_{i1} &= (\boldsymbol{X}'_{i2} - \boldsymbol{X}'_{i1}) \boldsymbol{\beta} + (c_i – c_i) + (\epsilon_{i2} – \epsilon_{i1}) \\
\text{ or: } \Delta Y_i &= \Delta \boldsymbol{X}_{i} \boldsymbol{\beta} + \Delta\epsilon_i
\end{align*}
$\rightarrow$ The unobserved heterogeneity $c_i$ drops out! (and also $\beta_0$)

\pause 
This is our “estimating equation”. \\
We can estimate $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ by OLS by regressing $\Delta Y_i$ on $\Delta \boldsymbol{X}_i$. \\
The estimator is unbiased if $E[\Delta\epsilon_i|\Delta \boldsymbol{X}_{i}] = 0$ (MLR.4 for this model).
\end{frame}


\begin{frame}{Within Transformation}
Option 3) The most common estimation approach is the \textit{within-transformation}.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item For all variables in your model, compute the time-mean for each unit $i$: \\
\hspace{+0.3cm} $\overline{Y_{i}} = \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^T Y_{it}$, $\overline{X}_{1i} = \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^T X_{1it}$, ...
\pause
    \item For each observation, deduct the time means from the actual values. Obtain the \textit{demeaned} variables $(Y_{it}-\overline{Y_{i}}),  (X_{1it}-\overline{X}_{1i}),...., (X_{kit}−-\overline{X}_{ki})$
    \pause
    \item  Regress  $(Y_{it}-\overline{Y_{i}})$ on $(X_{1it}-\overline{X}_{1i})$,..., $(X_{kit}-−\overline{X}_{ki})$
\end{enumerate}

\textit{Demeaned} Equation:
\begin{align*}
Y_{it}-\overline{Y}_{i} & = (\beta_0 -\beta_0) + \beta_1(X_{1it}-\overline{X}_{1i}) + ...+ \beta_k(X_{kit}-\overline{X_{ki}}) + \color{red}(c_i- c_i) +\color{black} (\epsilon_{it} -\overline{\epsilon}_i) \\
& =\beta_1(X_{1it}-\overline{X}_{1i}) + ...+ \beta_k(X_{kit}-\overline{X}_{ki}) + (\epsilon_{it}-\overline{\epsilon}_i) 
\end{align*}

$c_i$ drops out because there is no \textit{within-variation} in $c_i$! \\
Again, we can estimate this “demeaned equation” by OLS.
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]{Within Estimator -- Manual}
\footnotesize
<<include = FALSE>>=
meansx <- as.data.frame(tapply(dat$seatbelt_usage, dat$state, mean)) 
names(meansx) <- "means_x"
meansx$state <- rownames(meansx)
meansx
dat = merge(dat, meansx)
dat$fatal_demeaned = dat$fatality_rate - dat$means_x # demean variables
@

<<>>=
meansy <- as.data.frame(tapply(dat$fatality_rate, dat$state, mean)) # compute time means
names(meansy) <- "means_y"
meansy$state <- rownames(meansy)
meansy # time mean of dependent variable for each state
dat = merge(dat, meansy, by = "state")
dat$fatal_demeaned = dat$fatality_rate - dat$means_y # demean dependend variable
# same for regressor (computation ommited)
dat$seatbelt_demeaned = dat$seatbelt_usage -dat$means_x 
@

\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]{Within Estimator -- Manual}
\footnotesize
<<>>=
mod_within <- lm(fatal_demeaned ~ seatbelt_demeaned, data = dat) 
coeftest(mod_within, , vcov. = vcovHC(mod_within, "HC1"))
@
\small
We obtain the same slope coefficient estimate with the dummy variable and the within-transformation approaches.
\end{frame}



\begin{frame}[fragile]{Within Estimator with \texttt{fixest}}
We can directly implement the within-estimator in R using the command \alert{\texttt{feols}} from the package \alert{\texttt{fixest}}.

\small \color{gray}
\texttt{install.packages("fixest") \\
library(fixest) \\
fe\_mod <- feols(fatality\_rate $\sim$ seatbelt\_usage | state, data = dat) \\
summary(fe\_mod)
}


\texttt{
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
Fixed-effects: &state: 4 &&&\\
Standard-errors: &Clustered (state)&&& \\
&&& \\
      &         Estimate Std.& Error & t value &$Pr(>|t|)$    \\
seatbelt\_usage& -0.016942  & 0.003851 &-4.39955& 0.021769 *  \\   
 \end{tabular} }
\normalsize
\color{black}

Syntax is similar to  \texttt{estimatr::iv\_robust}: here the \textbf{fixed effects come after the $|$}

The command directly provides \textbf{cluster-robust standard errors}: they account for dependence between observations across time within a cross-sectional group (state).
\end{frame}


\begin{frame}{Limitations of Panel Data Estimators}
Unlike pooled OLS, % estimator: exploits deviations from the overall sample means of $Y$ and $X_j$  \\
 the within-estimator exploits only \textit{within-}variation for each unit $i$, i.e. deviations from the unit-specific mean. (like the other approaches we considered)

%Important limitation of these approaches: \\
\alert{\textbf{We cannot include time-invariant regressors}} when using these estimators.   They would drop out, because they are absorbed by the individual fixed effect.

Example: in a wage regression, we cannot estimate the slope coefficient on \textit{gender}. 

%(e.g. gender)
Even for variables with only little  time-variation, estimating effects might not work well (e.g. education only varies among very young individuals.) 
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Practice}
We are interested in the effect of union membership on the wage.
  \small  \begin{enumerate}
        \item Open the dataset \texttt{unions.csv}, a multi-year panel of workers with person identifier $nr$.
        
        \item Pooled OLS: Regress $lwage$ (log wage) on $educ$, $black$, $hisp$, $exper$ and its square, $union$ and all year dummies: $d82$, $d83$,  ...
        
        \item What is the effect of union membership according to this model? Why might the coefficient be inconsistent?
        
        \item What is the wage gap between white and black workers? How did wages change on average between 1982 and 1985?
        
        \item Use \texttt{feols} to take individual fixed effects into account. How does the estimated effect of union membership change?

        \item Why can you not estimate the black-white wage gap with the fixed effect estimator?

        \item  Keep only the years 1983 and 1987. Repeat the feols estimation. Then, add a regressor to estimate how the black-white gender gap $changed$ between these two years.

        \item Compute the first differences estimator. 
    \end{enumerate}
\end{frame}
